---
// Cmdr's accessibility conformance report, in the order and terms of ITI's VPAT 2.5Rev INT
// edition, for procurement and accessibility reviewers. Linked from `/trust` § Accessibility. Every
// row, the screen-reader checks still to run, and the product facts live in
// `src/lib/accessibility-report.ts`; the evidence behind each row is
// `docs/notes/accessibility-audit-2026-10.md`. "VPAT" is ITI's registered service mark: name the
// template only as "based on VPAT® 2.5Rev INT", never call this page a VPAT. Never claim WCAG
// conformance while any row says "Partially supports".
import LegalLayout from '../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro'
import {
    conformanceLabel,
    conformanceTerms,
    gapIssues,
    report,
    standards,
    voiceOverChecks,
    type CriterionRow,
} from '../../lib/accessibility-report'

const repo = 'https://github.com/vdavid/cmdr'
const issueUrl = (n: number) => `${repo}/issues/${n}`

const allRows = standards.flatMap((standard) => standard.tables.flatMap((table) => table.rows))
const notEvaluatedCount = allRows.filter((row) => row.conformance === 'not-evaluated').length
const pendingCount = allRows.filter((row) => row.pending?.length).length

const escapeHtml = (text: string) => text.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;')

/** The remarks cell: the remark, the pending-test note, and links to the issues behind it. One string, so no stray whitespace lands before the commas. */
const remarkHtml = (row: CriterionRow) => {
    const parts = [escapeHtml(row.remark)]
    if (row.pending?.length) parts.push('A screen-reader test is pending.')
    if (row.issues?.length) {
        const links = row.issues.map((n) => `<a href="${issueUrl(n)}">#${n}</a>`).join(', ')
        parts.push(`Tracked in ${links}.`)
    }
    return parts.join(' ')
}

const levelText = (row: CriterionRow) =>
    row.conformance === 'see-other' && row.seeLabel ? row.seeLabel : conformanceLabel[row.conformance]

const toc = [
    { id: 'details', label: 'Product and report' },
    { id: 'notes', label: 'Notes' },
    { id: 'methods', label: 'Evaluation methods' },
    { id: 'applicable-standards', label: 'Applicable standards' },
    { id: 'terms', label: 'Terms' },
    ...standards.map((standard) => ({ id: standard.id, label: standard.title })),
    { id: 'pending', label: 'Screen-reader test still to run' },
    { id: 'legal', label: 'Legal' },
]
---

<LegalLayout
    title="Accessibility conformance report"
    description="How Cmdr meets WCAG 2.2 Level A and AA, Revised Section 508, and EN 301 549, criterion by criterion, with known gaps. Based on VPAT® 2.5Rev INT, for procurement and accessibility reviewers."
    lastUpdated={report.date}
>
    <p>
        This is Cmdr's accessibility conformance report, based on {report.edition}. It's for procurement and
        accessibility teams who need to know how Cmdr works with assistive technology, criterion by criterion. Gaps
        link to their public GitHub issue, so you can follow each fix. To turn on Cmdr's accessibility features, see
        the <a href="/trust/accessibility-guide">accessibility guide</a>. For security, privacy, and IT questions, see
        the main <a href="/trust">trust and security page</a>.
    </p>

    <nav aria-label="On this page">
        <ul>
            {
                toc.map((item) => (
                    <li>
                        <a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a>
                    </li>
                ))
            }
        </ul>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="details">Product and report</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Name of product and version</strong>: {report.product}, version {report.version}</li>
        <li><strong>Report date</strong>: {report.date}</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Product description</strong>: Cmdr is a keyboard-first, two-pane file manager for macOS 12 Monterey
            and later. Its interface runs in Apple's WebKit web engine inside a native app, and reaches VoiceOver and
            other assistive technology through the macOS accessibility interface.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Contact information</strong>: <a href="mailto:hello@getcmdr.com">hello@getcmdr.com</a>, or Help &gt;
            Send feedback in the app. Accessibility questions and reports are welcome.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="notes">Notes</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Cmdr doesn't conform to WCAG 2.2 Level AA yet.</strong> Many criteria are "Partially supports", and
            the open issues below track the gaps.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The file list works with VoiceOver</strong>, the core of the product. A hands-on VoiceOver test
            heard it read the file under the cursor with its kind and position, which files are marked, and which pane
            and folder you're in, and heard in-app menus read every row, submenus included. Dialogs, Settings, and
            forms are built for screen readers too, and some of their VoiceOver checks are still to run.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>What works well today</strong>: everything core runs from the keyboard (every action has a
            remappable shortcut and a command palette entry); dialogs keep and return focus correctly; text contrast is
            checked automatically in light and dark mode with every macOS accent color; Cmdr follows the macOS text
            size, light and dark mode, accent color, Reduce motion, Reduce transparency, Increase contrast, and
            Differentiate without color settings; the menu bar and context menus are native macOS menus; and there's no
            audio, video, or flashing content.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>{notEvaluatedCount} rows say "Not evaluated yet"</strong>: the code review found them supported, but
            only a screen-reader test can confirm it. {pendingCount} rows in total wait on that test, and some
            "Partially supports" rows may drop to "Does not support" after it. This report will be updated with the
            results.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>WCAG applied to software</strong>: Cmdr is a desktop app, so WCAG is applied as the W3C's
            <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wcag2ict-22/">WCAG2ICT</a> guidance describes. Criteria that apply only to
            web pages or to sets of software are "Not applicable".
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Open issues behind the gaps:</p>
    <ul>
        {
            gapIssues.map((issue) => (
                <li>
                    <a href={issueUrl(issue.number)}>#{issue.number}</a>: {issue.title}
                </li>
            ))
        }
    </ul>

    <h2 id="methods">Evaluation methods used</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Code review</strong> of every part of the app: file panes, dialogs, notifications, forms, Settings,
            Search, the file viewer, and the queue.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Automated accessibility tests</strong>: about 920 axe-core test cases over the app's user interface
            components (WCAG 2.0, 2.1, and 2.2 Level A and AA rules), a check that every component has such a test, and
            11 end-to-end axe tests of the running app in light and dark mode. They run during development and again on
            GitHub after each change.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Contrast computation</strong>: an automated check computes the contrast of every text and
            background color pair in light and dark mode, with all nine macOS accent colors, every pane tint, and every
            file-list row state.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Simulated screen-reader tests</strong>: the file list, in-app menus, notifications, Search, and the
            Settings sidebar have automated tests of what a screen reader says as you move through them. They follow
            the ARIA specification, not VoiceOver itself.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Keyboard review</strong>: every action checked for a keyboard path, plus focus order, focus traps,
            and focus return in dialogs.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Screen-reader test</strong>: a hands-on VoiceOver test with real keys (macOS 27, VoiceOver 10)
            covered browsing folders, switching panes, marking files, in-app menus, notifications, the command palette,
            and parts of Search and Settings. Not done yet: the rest of that test,
            <a href="#pending">listed below</a>.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="applicable-standards">Applicable standards and guidelines</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Applicable standards" tabindex="0">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Standard or guideline</th>
                    <th scope="col">Included in report</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines 2.0, 2.1, and 2.2</td>
                    <td>Level A: yes. Level AA: yes. Level AAA: no.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Revised Section 508 standards, published by the US Access Board (2017)</td>
                    <td>Yes</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>EN 301 549 Accessibility requirements for ICT products and services, V3.1.1 (2019-11) and V3.2.1 (2021-03)</td>
                    <td>Yes</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="terms">Terms</h2>
    <ul>
        {
            conformanceTerms.map(({ term, meaning }) => (
                <li>
                    <span class={`level level-${term}`}>{conformanceLabel[term]}</span>: {meaning}
                </li>
            ))
        }
    </ul>

    {
        standards.map((standard) => (
            <section aria-labelledby={standard.id}>
                <h2 id={standard.id}>{standard.title}</h2>
                {standard.tables.map((table) => (
                    <>
                        <h3 id={table.id}>{table.title}</h3>
                        {table.note && <p>{table.note}</p>}
                        {table.rows.length > 0 && (
                            <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-labelledby={table.id} tabindex="0">
                                <table class="criteria">
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th scope="col">Criteria</th>
                                            <th scope="col">Conformance level</th>
                                            <th scope="col">Remarks and explanations</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        {table.rows.map((row) => (
                                            <tr>
                                                <th scope="row">
                                                    {row.id} {row.name}
                                                    {row.level && ` (Level ${row.level})`}
                                                </th>
                                                <td>
                                                    <span class={`level level-${row.conformance}`}>{levelText(row)}</span>
                                                </td>
                                                <td set:html={remarkHtml(row)} />
                                            </tr>
                                        ))}
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            </div>
                        )}
                    </>
                ))}
            </section>
        ))
    }

    <h2 id="pending">Screen-reader test still to run</h2>
    <p>
        These VoiceOver checks will fill in the rows marked "Not evaluated yet" and confirm or lower the
        "Partially supports" rows that wait on them:
    </p>
    <ul>
        {voiceOverChecks.map((check) => <li>{check.what}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="legal">Legal</h2>
    <p>
        This report describes Cmdr version {report.version} as of {report.date}, to the best of our knowledge. It isn't a
        warranty, and later versions may differ. The VPAT® name and template are registered service marks of the
        Information Technology Industry Council (ITI). This report follows the structure of ITI's VPAT® 2.5Rev INT
        template; ITI didn't review or endorse it.
    </p>
</LegalLayout>

<style>
    .table-scroll {
        margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
        overflow-x: auto;
        border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
        border-radius: 0.5rem;
    }

    .table-scroll:focus-visible {
        outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
        outline-offset: 2px;
    }

    table {
        width: 100%;
        min-width: 36rem;
        border-collapse: collapse;
        font-size: 0.875rem;
        line-height: 1.6;
        color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    }

    th,
    td {
        padding: 0.625rem 0.75rem;
        text-align: left;
        vertical-align: top;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    }

    tbody tr:last-child th,
    tbody tr:last-child td {
        border-bottom: none;
    }

    thead th {
        font-weight: 600;
        color: var(--color-text-primary);
        background: var(--color-surface);
    }

    .criteria tbody th {
        width: 30%;
        font-weight: 500;
        color: var(--color-text-primary);
    }

    .criteria td:nth-child(2) {
        width: 9rem;
        font-weight: 600;
        color: var(--color-text-primary);
    }

    /* Conformance badges, for scanning. The label keeps the primary text color (contrast in both themes), and the
       hue rides on a tinted background and a dot, so color is never the only signal. */
    .level {
        --level-hue: var(--color-text-tertiary);
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.375rem;
        padding: 0.125rem 0.5rem;
        border-radius: 999px;
        background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--level-hue) 16%, transparent);
        color: var(--color-text-primary);
        font-weight: 600;
        line-height: 1.5;
        white-space: nowrap;
    }

    .level::before {
        content: '';
        flex: none;
        width: 0.5rem;
        height: 0.5rem;
        border-radius: 50%;
        background: var(--level-hue);
    }

    .level-supports {
        --level-hue: var(--color-icon-yes);
    }

    .level-partially-supports {
        --level-hue: var(--color-icon-warn);
    }

    .level-does-not-support {
        --level-hue: var(--color-warning);
    }

    .level-not-evaluated {
        --level-hue: var(--color-icon-soon);
    }

    td a {
        color: var(--color-accent-text);
        text-decoration: underline;
        text-underline-offset: 2px;
    }
</style>
